-- Where two rules of the same name could each take the inputs a named site
-- writes, a TYPICALLY default does not decide between them. Each site below
-- is ambiguous, or an arity error, as it was before TYPICALLY, and says so.
-- (ok/typically-overload-fit.l4 has the sites where the written inputs do
-- settle the choice.)

-- 1. Each rule completes the site by a default of its own, and both take a
-- NUMBER for `base`.
GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 3
      base IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled MEANS rate TIMES base

GIVEN offset IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 7
      base IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled MEANS offset PLUS base

#EVAL scaled WITH base IS 10

-- 2. A third rule that takes a STRING for `base` cannot take the site, but the
-- other two still can, and neither is complete as written, so the third rule
-- does not make the call any less ambiguous. The first rule's default for
-- `rate` is not taken.
GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 3
      base IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled2 MEANS rate TIMES base

GIVEN base IS A NUMBER
      factor IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled2 MEANS base PLUS factor

GIVEN base IS A STRING
      other IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled2 MEANS other

#EVAL scaled2 WITH base IS 10

-- 3. A call that gives its inputs by position still gives all of them. Another
-- rule of the same name does not change that.
GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 3
      base IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled3 MEANS rate TIMES base

GIVEN base IS A STRING
      factor IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled3 MEANS factor

#EVAL scaled3 10
